# CHAPTER 21: THE HIGH TIDE OF IMPERIALISM

# Core Concept Definitions
new imperialism (new_imp) : 19th century European expansion into Asia/Africa driven by Industrial Revolution needs for raw materials and markets, distinct from earlier trade-focused expansion
colonial system (col_sys) : Administrative frameworks established by European powers to exploit natural resources and maintain control over subject territories
india under british raj (raj) : British colonial rule over Indian subcontinent from 1800s-1947, combining direct and indirect administration
southeast asia colonial (sea_col) : European domination of Southeast Asia by 1900 through strategic ports, plantation economies, and local elite collaboration
africa colonial (afr_col) : Rapid partition and colonization of Africa 1880s-1900, known as the "Scramble for Africa"
anticolonialism (anti_col) : Indigenous resistance movements and emerging nationalist responses to European colonial rule

# THE SPREAD OF COLONIAL RULE - CAUSES AND MOTIVES
new_imp < (What caused it?) The Industrial Revolution created insatiable demand for raw materials and export markets beyond domestic capacity
new_imp < (What was the economic motive?) European economies required colonies to prevent depression by absorbing surplus manufactured goods and supplying cheap materials
new_imp < (What was the political motive?) Social Darwinism convinced Europeans that national survival depended on proving fitness through colonial expansion
new_imp < (What was the moral justification?) "White Man's Burden" to spread Christianity, democracy, progress and rescue "primitive" peoples from poverty and disease
new_imp < (What was Cecil Rhodes' vision?) Secret society to extend British rule worldwide including Africa, Holy Land, South America, and even recover United States

# TACTICS OF COLONIAL EXPANSION
new_imp < (How did tactics differ from earlier periods?) Shift from trade footholds and coastal presence to direct territorial control and inland penetration after 1880
new_imp < (What technological advantages enabled conquest?) Steam navigation, quinine for malaria, Maxim guns and Gatling guns allowing small European forces to defeat larger armies
new_imp < (What was the timeline of intensification?) After 1880 competition became a "scramble" as all major powers plus US and Japan engaged in global land grab

# THE COLONIAL SYSTEM - ADMINISTRATIVE METHODS
col_sys < (What is indirect rule?) Governing through local elites who maintained authority in exchange for economic rewards and confirmation of status, used in princely states and Dutch East Indies
col_sys < (What is direct rule?) Removing local elites and replacing with officials from mother country, used where resistance was strong like Burma and some African territories
col_sys < (What is assimilation?) French policy to transform colonial societies into Western image with European institutions, theoretically offering equality
col_sys < (What is association?) British preference for preserving local traditions while collaborating with elites, treating subjects as culturally distinct
col_sys < (What philosophical justification underpinned both?) Social Darwinism providing pseudoscientific validity to "might makes right" and racial hierarchy

# INDIA UNDER THE BRITISH RAJ - REFORMS
raj < (What educational reforms were introduced?) British civil service examination, schools for Indian elites, first female medical student 1875, expanded girls' education for better wives/mothers
raj < (What social reforms were enacted?) Sati outlawed, widows permitted remarriage, thuggee brigandage suppressed
raj < (What infrastructure was built?) Railroads, telegraph, postal service introduced shortly after Britain, Calcutta-Delhi highway 1839, northern India rail network 1853
raj < (What economic development occurred?) Limited textile and jute manufacturing, 80 mills in Bombay by 1920s, but prevented broader industrialization

# INDIA - COSTS OF COLONIALISM
raj < (What was the economic cost?) British textiles destroyed Bengali textile industry, zamindar tax system displaced peasants and increased rural debt
raj < (What was the political cost?) No democratic institutions introduced, quote: democratic institutions "can no more be carried to India by Englishmen than they can carry ice in their luggage"
raj < (What was the psychological impact?) British arrogance wounded Indian pride, educated Indians developed confused cultural loyalties, "Indian in blood, English in taste"
raj < (What was Thomas Macaulay's policy?) Created English-educated elite class to serve as interpreters between British and millions they governed

# SEPOY REBELLION 1857
sepoy rebellion 1857 (sepoy) : Mutiny of Indian troops triggered by Enfield rifle cartridges violating Hindu and Muslim religious beliefs
raj <caused> sepoy
sepoy < (What triggered it?) New Enfield rifle cartridges greased with cow fat and pig lard violated religious prohibitions requiring biting cartridge
sepoy < (Why did it fail?) Lacked clear goals, Hindu-Muslim discord prevented coordination, British forces supplemented by loyalist Sikh and Gurkha units
sepoy < (What were the consequences?) Reduced proportion of Indian troops, preference for "loyal" ethnic groups, final suppression of Mughal Dynasty, transferred administration to British Crown

# SOUTHEAST ASIA COLONIAL POSSESSIONS
sea_col < (What did France control?) Indochina Union: Vietnam (attacked 1858), Cambodia (1863), Laos (1893) - southern Vietnam direct rule, northern protectorate
sea_col < (What did Britain control?) Malaya, Singapore (founded 1819 by Stamford Raffles), Burma (attacked 1826) - Singapore was strategic port for China trade
sea_col < (What did the Netherlands control?) Dutch East Indies (early 1600s) with indirect rule through indigenous aristocracy collecting taxes
sea_col < (What did Spain/US control?) Philippines (Spanish 1521, US after 1898 Spanish-American War) as stepping-stone to China trade
sea_col < (Who remained independent?) Thailand through astute diplomacy of Kings Mongkut and Chulalongkorn, neutralized as buffer zone 1896

# SOUTHEAST ASIA - NATURE OF COLONIAL RULE
sea_col < (What was the primary goal?) Economic profit through cash crop exports: teakwood, rubber, tin, spices, tea, coffee, palm oil, sugar, copra
sea_col < (What labor system was used?) Plantation agriculture with forced recruitment ("shanghaied"), inhumane conditions, high death rates
sea_col < (What administrative approach?) Indirect rule preferred as less costly and culturally less corrosive, working with local collaborators
sea_col < (What democratic reforms?) Legislative councils initially European-only, slow inclusion of conservative indigenous elites, fear of creating educated unemployed rebels
sea_col < (What was the population impact?) Improved sanitation caused dramatic growth (Java 1M to 40M) leading to rural poverty and urban migration

# AFRICA - FROM SLAVE TRADE TO LEGITIMATE TRADE
afr_col < (What preceded formal colonization?) Decline of Atlantic slave trade (illegal in Britain 1808, US 1863) replaced by "legitimate trade" in peanuts, timber, palm oil
afr_col < (Where were early settlements?) British Gold Coast and Sierra Leone for freed slaves, Liberia with US assistance, French Senegal for peanut plantations
afr_col < (What was informal empire?) Areas like Egypt, lower Nigeria under loose protectorates without formal annexation before 1880s

# THE SCRAMBLE FOR AFRICA - TRIGGERS
scramble for africa (scramble) : Rapid European partition of Africa 1880s-1900 triggered by competition and King Leopold's Congo claim
afr_col <manifested_as> scramble
scramble < (What triggered it?) King Leopold II of Belgium claimed Congo for personal enrichment under guise of missionary activity
scramble < (What was the economic trigger?) Discovery of gold and diamonds in Transvaal, copper in Belgian Congo
scramble < (What was the strategic trigger?) Need to protect Suez Canal (completed 1869) as lifeline to India
scramble < (What technological factor enabled it?) Quinine discovery dramatically reduced European mortality in tropical Africa

# REGULATING THE SCRAMBLE
berlin conference 1884 (berlin) : Meeting establishing rules for African colonization to prevent European wars
scramble <regulated_by> berlin
berlin < (What principles were established?) Effective occupation required for recognition, free commerce in Congo and Niger, further efforts to end slave trade
berlin < (What was the outcome?) No African delegates present, rules recognized inevitability of imperialism while trying to manage competition
berlin < (What was the Fashoda Incident?) 1898 confrontation between Britain and France in Sudan, French backed down confirming British Nile control

# British Strategy in Africa
british nile strategy (nile_strat) : British effort to control Nile Valley to protect Suez Canal from French threat
scramble <motivated_by> nile_strat
nile_strat < (Why was Egypt crucial?) Muhammad Ali's modernization, canal construction left Egypt bankrupt and vulnerable to British financial control 1881
nile_strat < (What was the Mahdi Revolt?) 1881-85 Sudanese religious uprising led by Muhammad Ahmad defeating General Gordon at Khartoum
nile_strat < (What was the outcome?) British consolidated control over entire Nile Valley after Mahdi threat and Fashoda

# ETHIOPIA'S EXCEPTIONAL RESISTANCE
ethiopian independence (ethiopia) : Ethiopia remained independent after defeating Italy at Battle of Adowa 1896
scramble <defied_by> ethiopia
ethiopia < (How did they achieve it?) Emperor Menelik modernized army and purchased modern European weapons
ethiopia < (What was the battle?) Battle of Adowa 1896 destroyed Italian invasion force
ethiopia < (What was the significance?) Only African nation besides Liberia to preserve independence in colonial era

# SOUTH AFRICA: BOERS, BRITISH, AND ZULUS
south africa conflict (sa_conflict) : Three-way struggle between Boers, British, and Zulus for control of southern Africa
scramble <manifested_as> sa_conflict
sa_conflict < (Who were the Boers?) Afrikaans-speaking descendants of Dutch settlers who migrated east in Great Trek 1830s
sa_conflict < (Why did Boers migrate?) To escape British abolition of slavery 1834 and British sympathy for African rights
sa_conflict < (What was the Zulu resistance?) Bantu people led by Shaka fought bitter wars against Boers and British, subdued by century's end
sa_conflict < (What caused the Boer War?) Cecil Rhodes attempted to seize Transvaal's gold/diamonds, war 1899-1902 ended with British victory
sa_conflict < (What was the outcome?) Union of South Africa 1910 with white-only self-government, divided between English and Afrikaner populations

# COLONIAL RULE IN AFRICA - METHODS
afr_col < (What was British indirect rule?) Preserving African political traditions through local chiefs with British district officers as intermediaries, used in Nigeria
afr_col < (What was French direct rule?) Centralized system with governor-general, commissioners, African administrators required to be conversant in French
afr_col < (What was French assimilation policy?) Goal to assimilate Africans into French culture theoretically offering equality and participation in French politics
afr_col < (What was high colonialism?) Post-WWI phase as "sacred trust" with more social services but increased segregation and race consciousness
afr_col < (What was the impact on women?) Mixed: ended forced marriage/genital mutilation, but European gender attitudes restricted women's traditional roles

# THE EMERGENCE OF ANTICOLONIALISM
anti_col < (What was traditional resistance?) Initial defense of traditional institutions by existing ruling classes and peasant revolts
anti_col < (What forms did resistance take?) Guerrilla warfare, religious movements, peasant uprisings, collaboration vs resistance dilemmas
anti_col < (What was the path to nationalism?) Colonial borders created artificial nations, Western ideas of citizenship spread, resentment of racial superiority unified elites
anti_col < (What was the role of education?) Western-educated lawyers, teachers, journalists became vanguard of nationalist movements

# MAJOR RESISTANCE MOVEMENTS
anti_col < (What was the Sepoy Rebellion?) Largest uprising against European authority in mid-19th century, defeated but frightened British into reforms
anti_col < (What was the Mahdi Revolt?) Sudanese religious resistance that temporarily defeated British at Khartoum 1885
anti_col < (What was the Ashanti resistance?) West African struggle against British with broad popular support but ultimately defeated
anti_col < (What was the Can Vuong movement?) Vietnamese "Save the King" organization continuing resistance after monarchy surrendered

# COLLABORATION VS RESISTANCE DILEMMA
collaboration debate (collab) : Choice between resisting colonizers or cooperating for pragmatic reasons dividing Asian/African elites
anti_col <exemplified_by> collab
collab < (What was Hoang Cao Khai's argument?) Resistance is futile, causes unnecessary suffering, should accept reality for people's sake
collab < (What was Phan Dinh Phung's response?) Loyalty to king and country based on five moral obligations, resistance is righteous duty
collab < (What was Ram Mohan Roy's approach?) Bengali brahmin who founded Brahmo Samaj to defend Hindu values while accepting useful European ideas

# FINAL ASSESSMENT - BALANCE SHEET
colonial impact (col_imp) : Complex legacy with both destructive exploitation and unintended modernizing effects
new_imp <had_consequences> col_imp
col_imp < (What were positive effects?) Modern infrastructure, global trade networks, democratic ideas introduced, women's rights awareness, potential for future development
col_imp < (What were negative effects?) Economic exploitation, destruction of local industries, racial discrimination, prevented local development, brutally applied
col_imp < (What was fundamental weakness?) Based entirely on self-interest of colonial powers, colonial peoples' needs always subordinate, prevented self-determination
col_imp < (What was the tragedy?) Age-old societies squeezed of resources under false "civilizing mission," suffering for nothing rather than through colonial era

# KEY HISTORICAL FIGURES
cecil rhodes (rhodes) : British empire builder who wanted to extend British rule worldwide and make wars impossible
king leopold ii (leopold) : Belgian king whose personal Congo claim triggered scramble, used brutal forced labor for rubber
thomas babington macaulay (macaulay) : British official who created English-educated Indian elite "interpreters" between rulers and ruled
david livingstone (livingstone) : Scottish missionary who opposed slavery and advocated "3 Cs": Christianity, Commerce, Civilization
muhammad ali (muhammad_ali) : Ottoman officer who modernized Egypt, built Suez Canal, threatened Ottoman Empire
menelik ii (menelik) : Ethiopian emperor who modernized army and preserved independence at Battle of Adowa
alfred de leseps (leseps) : French entrepreneur who built Suez Canal 1869, left Egypt bankrupt
general charles gordon (gordon) : British general killed by Mahdi at Khartoum 1885, symbol of imperial overreach
jules ferry (ferry) : French politician who warned abstinence would make France a third-rate power
karl pearson (pearson) : British social Darwinist who argued superior races must supplant inferior ones
rudyard kipling (kipling) : Poet who justified imperialism as "White Man's Burden" to civilize "half-devil" peoples
edmund morel (morel) : British journalist who exposed brutal exploitation in Belgian Congo with "Black Man's Burden"
henry curzon (curzon) : British official who called empire "greatest instrument for good under Providence"
albert sarrauat (sarrauat) : French governor who justified colonialism as redistributing earth's wealth for universal life
eduard douwes dekker (dekker) : Dutch official who exposed Javanese exploitation in "Max Havelaar"
ram mohan roy (roy) : Bengali brahmin who founded Brahmo Samaj, first stirrings of Indian nationalism

# CAUSAL CHAINS
industrial revolution (ind_rev) : 19th century mechanization and factory production
ind_rev <caused> new_imp
new_imp <had_consequences> col_sys
col_sys <implemented_in> raj
col_sys <implemented_in> sea_col
col_sys <implemented_in> afr_col
raj <generated> sepoy
sepoy <led_to> anti_col
scramble <triggered_by> leopold
berlin <attempted_to_regulate> scramble
nile_strat <required> suez
suez <motivated> brit_egypt
brit_egypt <provoked> mahdi
mahdi <inspired> anti_col
ethiopia <demonstrated> anti_col
sa_conflict <produced> union_south_africa
union_south_africa <institutionalized> racial_segregation

# CHRONOLOGY ANCHORS
1819 : Stamford Raffles founded Singapore
1826 : British attacked lower Burma
1853 : British rail network opened in northern India
1857 : Sepoy Rebellion
1858 : France attacked Vietnam
1859 : French created Indochina Union
1863 : French protectorate over Cambodia
1869 : Suez Canal completed
1873 : Zanzibar slave market closed
1874 : British established Gold Coast colony
1881 : British informal protectorate over Egypt
1884 : Berlin Conference on Africa
1885 : Mahdi captured Khartoum killing General Gordon
1893 : French protectorate over Laos
1896 : Britain and France agreed to neutralize Thailand; Ethiopia won at Adowa
1898 : Commodore Dewey defeated Spanish fleet in Manila Bay; US took Philippines; Fashoda Incident
1899 : Boer War began
1900 : French created Indochinese Union
1902 : Boer War ended
1904 : Casement Commission report on Belgian Congo
1910 : Union of South Africa established
